"""Integrate experiment and appendix prose without changing results."""
from pathlib import Path
import json,re
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];OUT=ROOT/'review-revision/sentence-review-implementation'
main=(ROOT/'manuscript/main.tex').read_text();app=(ROOT/'manuscript/appendix.tex').read_text()
actions=json.loads((OUT/'text-actions.json').read_text())
def change(which,old,new,ids):
    global main,app
    s=main if which=='main' else app
    assert s.count(old)==1,(which,ids,old[:80],s.count(old))
    s=s.replace(old,new)
    if which=='main':main=s
    else:app=s
    actions.append({'file':'manuscript/'+('main.tex' if which=='main' else 'appendix.tex'),'review_ids':ids,'old':old,'new':new})
def paragraph(which,start,new,ids):
    s=main if which=='main' else app
    at=s.index('\n'+start)+1;old=s[at:].split('\n\n')[0];change(which,old,new,ids)

paragraph('main','The processed RewardBench',r'The processed RewardBench~2 panel contains 1,723 prompts, each with four completions scored by 14 reward models \citep{malik2026rewardbench2}. WMT23 en-de contains 557 source segments, 13 output streams and 24 distinct reference-based metrics \citep{freitag2023wmt23}. A twelve-stream variant removes the output named \texttt{synthetic\_ref} before refitting the protocol. All source segments remain; the output population and fitted score maps change.',[61])
paragraph('main','The first seven evaluators',r'The first seven evaluators in the fixed Appendix~\ref{app:protocol} order form the base pool; the others are candidates: $T=7$ for RewardBench and $T=17$ for both WMT23 panels. Each of 40 seeded splits partitions whole prompt or source groups into reference, calibration and test thirds. Selection shares one churn budget across the test batch, with no per-group return quota. Maps and cutoff use reference data; CRC and grouped SCoRE use calibration data. Both pooling rules run at $q\in\{.05,.10,.20,.30\}$, with main budget $\alpha=.10$ and sensitivity at $.02,.05,.15,.20$. The grid gives $3\times2\times4\times40\times5=4{,}800$ configurations, sharing 3,840 budget-independent acquisition orders with separate stops.',[62,63])
paragraph('main','The main stop uses',r'The primary stop is $V^+-V^-\le\lceil.02|S_0(P)|\rceil$, measured in retained outputs; exact stopping uses zero gap. At $\alpha=.10$, 960 configurations yield 3,840 paths with checkpoints at 0, 10, 25, 50 and 100\% of active entries. Earlier exact states are carried forward to later allowances. Overlapping splits supply descriptive panel summaries.',[64])
paragraph('main','A query inspects one cached',r'Queries reveal cached candidate test scores; evaluators are not rerun. Reference and base-pool scoring are counted separately. Acquisition counts exclude the earlier full-panel WMT23 correlation check, so they describe information use after panel construction. Appendix~\ref{app:protocol} gives seeds, identities, ties and accounting.',[65])
change('main',r'\subsection{Observable-risk controls}',r'\subsection{Retention baselines and risk guarantees}',[66])
paragraph('main','Selected coverage is',r'Selected coverage is $K/|S_0|$, with $K$ the returned count. At $q=.05$, $\alpha=.10$ and median pooling, zero-query certification retains \rbZeroCov\% of selected RewardBench outputs and \wmtZeroCov\% on thirteen-stream WMT23; full-information optima reach \rbOptCov\% and \wmtOptCov\%. In twelve-stream WMT23, zero-query coverage is \exclZeroCov\%, close to its \exclOptCov\% optimum, leaving little retention to gain by querying (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:baselines}).',[67])
paragraph('main','A maximum-cardinality mask',r'Baselines optimize different objects. All-decision cardinality can spend budget on nonselected outputs; selected retention allocates it to returned outputs. Uniform thresholds also include cost ties together and may pay for nonselected flips. Coverage differences therefore cannot be attributed to acquisition order alone. Fixed-count retention changes the selection rule (Appendix~\ref{app:risk-interpretation}).',[68])
paragraph('main','Envelope CRC calibrates',r'Envelope CRC calibrates the itemwise loss $d_i=F_i/H$ with correction $B\lambda$, $B=N/(2k)$ \citep{yeh2025conformal}. Its expectation guarantee permits individual batch violations. When its chosen threshold is feasible on the current batch, the best batch-feasible threshold in the same nested family retains at least as many decisions as that CRC threshold. Appendix~\ref{app:baselines} specifies these controls.',[69])
paragraph('main','Grouped SCoRE-MDR targets',r'Grouped SCoRE-MDR targets the same churn loss on equal-size output groups using reference-fitted priorities and fully scored calibration groups. At $q=.05$, $\alpha=.10$, RewardBench selected coverage is \scoreRbMedianCov\% under median and \scoreRbMeanCov\% under mean, without candidate test scoring for selection. Its expectation guarantee assumes conditional whole-group exchangeability and accepts groups together. Appendix~\ref{app:review-score} gives the reduction and scoring costs; it is not applied to unequal-size WMT24 documents.',[70])
paragraph('main','Table~\\ref{tab:stopping} separates',r'At $q=.05$, $\alpha=.10$, Table~\ref{tab:stopping} separates zero-query stops from active inspection; Appendix Figure~\ref{fig:frontiers} plots safe coverage and the full-information optimum. Median reference priority inspects 61.4\% of active entries on RewardBench and 45.1\% on thirteen-stream WMT23, versus 63.8\% and 57.9\% for fixed order. It uses strictly fewer queries than all three other strategies simultaneously on 21/40 and 31/40 splits. Twelve-stream WMT23 stops without queries on 92.5\% of splits. Mean reference priority inspects 85.4--86.5\% of active entries across these panels.',[71])
paragraph('main','Zero-query coverage is not invariance',r'Zero-query coverage includes more than invariant outputs. At this depth, invariant outputs account for 46.6\%, 54.4\% and 75.1\% of the selected sets in RewardBench and the thirteen- and twelve-stream WMT23 panels. Noninvariant outputs fitting the budget add 34.3, 30.6 and 24.1 percentage points. The components sum to zero-query coverage before rounding; zero-query stopping rates of 0, 10 and 92.5\% instead measure whether the initial gap meets tolerance. Appendix~\ref{app:zero-decomposition} covers all panels, pooling rules and depths.',[72])
paragraph('main','Reference priority is not uniformly',r'Reference priority is not uniformly best across depths. At $q=.30$ with mean pooling on thirteen-stream WMT23, it averages 2,363.8 queries versus 1,800.6 for largest width. Appendix Table~\ref{tab:query-prefix-example} illustrates the ordering difference at fixed budget and tolerance on one split; it does not identify the cause. Tables~\ref{tab:all-tolerant} and~\ref{tab:all-exact} give all depths and pooling rules.',[73,74])
paragraph('main','Table~\\ref{tab:certificates} relates',r'Table~\ref{tab:certificates} compares exact online inspection with Theorem~\ref{thm:exact}\textquotesingle s offline witness. Agreements can dominate when nearly all boundary rows must be retained; oppositions prove that no larger retained set is feasible. On positive-witness splits, the median reference query-to-$Q_{\rm exact}$ ratio is 1.968 for RewardBench and 1.662 for thirteen-stream WMT23. Appendix~\ref{app:granularity} reports tails and zero witnesses. Positive-gap comparisons instead use $Q_\varepsilon$ (Appendix~\ref{app:tolerance}).',[75])
paragraph('main','Figure~\\ref{fig:review-budgets} tests',r'Figure~\ref{fig:review-budgets} varies $\alpha$ at fixed loss, cutoff and acquisition order. Inspection is nonmonotone: median reference priority inspects \rbMiddleActive\% and \wmtMiddleActive\% of active entries on RewardBench and thirteen-stream WMT23 at $\alpha=.05$, exceeding its fractions at both $.02$ and $.10$. Exact witnesses describe required agreement and opposition evidence; online counts also depend on its discovery order.',[76])
paragraph('main','To test sensitivity to evaluator',r'For composition sensitivity, we sample 20 distinct seven-member pools per panel, using all remaining evaluators as candidates. Each WMT23 panel also has a metadata-defined pool with three surface-overlap metrics and four others. Refitting cutoff and priorities over all depths, pooling rules and 40 splits gives 19,840 configurations and 79,360 paths (Appendix~\ref{app:review-composition}). For thirteen-stream WMT23 at $q=.05$ under median pooling, we first average reference active inspection over splits within each random pool. Across pools, the median is \compWmtMedianActive\%, with a descriptive 10th--90th percentile range of \compWmtMedianLow--\compWmtMedianHigh\%. Evaluator composition substantially changes query requirements in these panels.',[77])
paragraph('main','The risk objective and admission',r'We fixed the risk objective and panel-inclusion criteria before inspecting WMT24 results. The official en-de, en-es and ja-zh panels \citep{freitag2024wmt24} contain 998, 998 and 722 source paragraphs; 26, 23 and 22 MT output streams; and 21, 21 and 22 distinct default-reference metrics, respectively. Human references are excluded as outputs. Alphabetical order assigns the first seven metrics to the pool and the rest to candidates. Documents stay intact across splits. The same four acquisition rules, features, depths and stopping criteria run at $\alpha=.10$ on 960 configurations. Appendix~\ref{app:review-quality} discloses a numerical tie-handling repair made after an exact-replay failure.',[78,79])
paragraph('main','Table~\\ref{tab:review-external} shows',r'At $q=.05$, median zero-query coverage is already 98.1--99.8\% (Table~\ref{tab:review-external}). Reference active inspection is \deMedianActive\%, \esMedianActive\% and \zhMedianActive\% in en-de, en-es and ja-zh, versus \deMeanActive\%, \esMeanActive\% and \zhMeanActive\% under mean pooling. Invariant outputs contribute 77.8--85.3\% of median selected sets; budget-admissible noninvariant outputs add 14.2--20.3 points. Low median query counts thus coincide with high initial coverage.',[80])
change('main',r'\subsection{Quality and the meaning of savings}',r'\subsection{Post-selection quality diagnostics}',[81])
paragraph('main','We use native RewardBench',r'We assess preference precision using native RewardBench labels. For each returned count $K$, a comparator selects the $K$ highest base-consensus outputs with label-blind ties. It matches count, not a stability certificate. Median reference precision minus comparator precision averages $-0.0009$, $-0.0046$, $-0.0027$ and $-0.0003$ over the four depths. These differences do not support improved preference precision. Appendix~\ref{app:quality} reports all strategies and depths.',[82])
paragraph('main','The WMT23 score panels used',r'WMT23 has no human-quality labels. WMT24 MQM covers 31.9\%, 38.0\% and 49.4\% of admitted en-de, en-es and ja-zh outputs, respectively. Labels are used after masks and paths are fixed. Appendix~\ref{app:review-quality} gives observed-label means and comparisons matched by returned or annotated count. Differences vary in sign and describe only annotated outputs; missing labels are not imputed. A separate sentinel diagnostic examines discrimination between translations of the same source. Source-only and reference-only probes \citep{perrella2024sentinel} have positive pooled MQM correlations while tying all systems within each paragraph (Table~\ref{tab:review-sentinel}).',[83,84])
paragraph('main','Full, half and quarter reference',r'We evaluate six reference protocols: full, half and quarter reference-group sizes crossed with right and midrank empirical cumulative distribution functions (ECDFs). Test groups stay fixed; every depth and pooling rule runs. At $q=.05$, thirteen-stream WMT23 median zero-query coverage rises from 84.9\% under full-reference right ECDF to 99.8\% under midrank, while reference active inspection falls from 45.1\% to 0.3\%. Mean splitwise baseline-set Jaccard is 0.77. This changes the selected set and protocol, rather than isolating better acquisition for a fixed list (Appendix~\ref{app:mapping-column}).',[85,86])
paragraph('main','Whole-evaluator queries reveal',r'We next compare entry and whole-evaluator queries. At the original RewardBench $q=.05$ positive-gap protocol, all fractions use the full panel $NT$: reference entries inspect 1.77\% under median and 4.52\% under mean, versus 71.8\% and 96.1\% for columns. Entry paths still access all seven candidates. At exact median stopping, the minimum RewardBench column witness averages \granRbColumns{} columns (\granRbMinimum\% of scores), versus \granRbOnlineColumns{} for reference order; the thirteen-stream WMT23 minimum averages \granWmtMinimum\%. Tables~\ref{tab:column-witness-summary} and~\ref{tab:column-witness-all} separate certificate size from discovery overhead.',[87,88])
change('main',r'Across all six panels, top-5\% reference mean inspection remains 72.4--76.2\% at 10\% nominal tolerance (Appendix~\ref{app:tolerance}). Appendix~\ref{app:practical} connects tolerance, mapping and oracle choice to scoring workload.',r'Controlled panels isolate column sharing at identical row costs. With $m=128,T=12,d=1,b=0$, every layout has minimum entry certificate size 128. Shared support requires one column; cyclic and all eight sampled independent supports require twelve (Appendix~\ref{app:synthetic-sharing}). At 10\% nominal tolerance, tested reference mean paths still inspect 72.4--76.2\% of active entries across all six NLP panels (Appendix~\ref{app:tolerance}). Appendix~\ref{app:practical} relates protocol choices to scoring workload.',[170])
paragraph('main','Partial evaluator scores bound',r'We formulate stable retained-output selection as a budget problem under a declared evaluator-replacement family. Partial observations bound the feasible count and its regret. Median entry certificates balance feasibility and exclusion evidence at exact and positive tolerance; column certificates additionally depend on shared evidence. NLP replays and controlled panels distinguish these requirements from acquisition and selection-protocol effects. Quality and physical scoring cost require separate evidence.',[89])
paragraph('main','The certificates cover the declared',r'The certificates cover uniformly weighted single-member replacements from the declared family. Outputs added after review are outside the fixed mask, and nominal $2k$ normalization does not enforce a final count of $k$. The entry-witness formulas assume unit-cost candidate--output queries and Boolean completions without cross-entry constraints. Columns reveal an evaluator\textquotesingle s whole batch under the same completion model. Guaranteed relations between missing scores define a different certification problem.',[90])
paragraph('main','RewardBench and WMT23 were exposed',r'RewardBench and WMT23 were used during method development. WMT24 results had not been inspected when fixing the risk objective and panel-inclusion criteria, but evaluator training data may overlap with the benchmark, and some released meta-metrics use human-score calibration. Sampled pools do not represent all future evaluators; reference priority has no online competitive guarantee. Incomplete MQM limits quality diagnostics to observed labels on the current benchmark. Model execution cost is not measured.',[91,92])
paragraph('main','The NLP experiments use existing',r'The experiments reuse numerical evaluator panels and identifiers or generate controlled numerical scores; they collect no new human labels or language responses. Stable selection can preserve evaluator errors, so a certificate does not endorse retained content. Dataset sources and acquisition instructions are documented in \texttt{DATA\_STATEMENT\_CURRENT.md}, included in the prepared source and code packages. Complete derived-score redistribution permission remains unresolved; the data archive remains local and has not been publicly released.',[93,94])

# Appendix edits retain the original mathematical derivations and identities.
change('app','With a nonempty reference empirical CDF, every reference score maps above zero, so a reference-fitted cutoff satisfies', 'Under the nonempty reference ECDF conventions used here, every reference score maps into $(0,1]$, so a cutoff fitted from reference consensus satisfies',[96])
paragraph('app','For mean pooling, each replacement',r'For mean pooling, each replacement mean is nondecreasing in candidate score $x$. On a selected row, increasing $x$ cannot create an exclusion, so $f_i(x)$ is nonincreasing, with maximum $f_i(0)$. At $x=1$, the candidate score is at least every base score it can replace; all replacement means remain at or above the cutoff, giving $f_i(1)=0$. On an unselected row, $f_i(x)$ is nondecreasing, with maximum $f_i(1)$ and $f_i(0)=0$. The same argument covers ties because membership always uses $\ge$.',[97])
paragraph('app','The interval after partial observation',r'Each row\textquotesingle s cost sums observed flip counts and unobserved counts bounded by zero and $p_i$. With no cross-entry constraints, joint endpoint assignments realize both $\ell$ and $u$. Consequently, $V(\ell)$ is the largest full-information retention capacity among consistent completions, and $V(u)$ the smallest. These extrema are sharp in the stated model. Known candidate functions, score correlations and raw-score restrictions are not used to reduce the completion set.',[98])
paragraph('app','For numerical replay,',r'The replay rebuilds replacement score vectors and uses the same left-to-right mean reduction for complete-matrix evaluation and individual queries. It does not subtract a member from a previously rounded sum, keeping observed and endpoint flip calculations numerically consistent. For rational budget $\alpha=a/d$, it computes $b_H=\lfloor2kMTa/d\rfloor$ by exact integer division.',[99,100])
change('app',r'If an $L$-mask is truly feasible, its actual agreement count is $LT-\sum_{i\in C}z_i\ge LT-b$. An informed witness attains the lower bound by revealing any $\max(0,LT-b)$ agreement cells in this mask, making its upper cost at most $b$. If no true-feasible $L$-mask exists, no observation can prove one safe, and the target has infinite witness cost.',r'A truly feasible set $C$ of $L$ boundary rows contains $LT-\sum_{i\in C}z_i\ge LT-b$ agreement cells. Revealing any $\max(0,LT-b)$ of them makes its upper cost at most $b$, attaining the lower bound. If no truly feasible $L$-row set exists, no observations can certify one; the target has infinite witness cost.',[101])
change('app',r'Otherwise, fill all unknown cells in a minimum-lower-cost $p$-mask with agreements. That consistent completion makes the mask feasible. Coordinate domination implies order-statistic domination: $x_{(i)}\le z_{(i)}$. Let $t=x_{(p)}$. Then',r'Otherwise, choose the $p$ rows with the smallest observed opposition counts and complete their unknown cells with agreements. This makes that set feasible in a consistent completion. Since $x_i\le z_i$ for every row, the sorted values satisfy $x_{(i)}\le z_{(i)}$. Let $t=x_{(p)}$. Then',[102])
change('app',r'The definition of $t^*$ ensures that at least one of these counts equals $t^*$: a vector capped below that level could not sum to $B_0$.',r'These counts are integers. If all were below $t^*$, their sum would be at most $D(t^*-1)<B_0$; at least one must therefore equal $t^*$.',[103])
paragraph('app','The safety witness can be taken',r'Choose safety agreements from any truly optimal set of $L$ rows. Their cells are disjoint from the exclusion witness\textquotesingle s opposition cells. Revealing the union certifies feasibility and rules out any feasible $p$-row set under every completion, at the sum of the two costs. Every exact certificate must meet both lower bounds, proving Equation~\ref{eq:exact}.',[104])
change('app','The integer retention gap does not generally have diminishing query gains.','Reduction in the integer retention gap need not have diminishing returns.',[105])
change('app','Thus the marginal value of the second agreement is larger after the first has been observed.','The second agreement has a larger marginal gap reduction after the first is observed.',[105])
paragraph('app','The frozen score panels contain',r'The frozen panels contain numerical scores and identifiers. RewardBench excludes ties and prompts without exactly four completions; each retained prompt forms a four-output group. For each evaluator, alignment checks match the native prompt and every completion to the corresponding model-formatted input, preserving evaluator-specific formatting. Aligned records use native order: chosen completion first, then three rejected completions. Repeated result identifiers follow the original builder\textquotesingle s last-eligible-occurrence policy. WMT retains distinct reference-based metrics under a common reference configuration and removes byte-identical duplicates.',[106,107])
change('app','This full-panel check neither changes score signs nor removes metrics on this criterion; a nonpositive correlation would reject the build.','A nonpositive correlation would reject the build. Since all are positive, this check changes neither score signs nor metric inclusion.',[108])
change('app','574/574 prompts','574 reference and 574 calibration prompts',[109]) if '574/574 prompts' in app else None
change('app','185/185 source groups','185 reference and 185 calibration source groups',[109]) if '185/185 source groups' in app else None
change('app','The results-dataset revision does not establish the corresponding model-weight checkpoints.','The results-dataset revision alone does not identify the corresponding model-weight checkpoints.',[110]) if 'The results-dataset revision does not establish the corresponding model-weight checkpoints.' in app else None
